WEBVTT

1.1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.080
How did a fabric woven in China end up in the wardrobes of Roman senators, six thousand kilometers away?

2.1
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:15.891
Starting around one hundred thirty BC, under the Han dynasty, traders began linking China to the Mediterranean.

2.2
00:00:15.891 --> 00:00:20.707
It was never one road, but a web of routes across deserts and mountains.

3.1
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:29.732
Here is the main route, from Chang'an in China, through Central Asia and Persia, to the ports of the Mediterranean.

4.1
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.456
Almost nobody traveled the whole way.

4.2
00:00:33.456 --> 00:00:38.804
Goods passed hand to hand, through merchant after merchant, and each one took a cut.

5.1
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:46.656
Silk was the star, but spices, paper, glass, horses and precious metals moved too.

6.1
00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:50.671
Ideas rode along with the caravans.

6.2
00:00:50.671 --> 00:00:58.737
Buddhism spread from India into China, paper and printing moved west, and so did gunpowder and the compass.

7.1
00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:01.773
Trade also carried danger.

7.2
00:01:01.773 --> 00:01:09.469
In the thirteen forties, the Black Death likely spread along these same routes and reached Europe in thirteen forty-seven.

8.1
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:20.275
The routes faded after the fourteen hundreds, as Ottoman control grew and ships opened sea routes around Africa and across the oceans.

9.1
00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:26.873
So that Roman silk? It passed through dozens of hands over thousands of kilometers.

9.2
00:01:26.873 --> 00:01:32.707
The Silk Road was a chain of exchange, and it connected the world long before the modern age.
